WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.022
- This is the Linby Trail,

00:00:05.022 --> 00:00:08.130
near the little village of Linby
in Nottinghamshire, in England.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.890
I'm filming on the trail
because it'd be really weird

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.910
to do a walk-and-talk
to a camera on a stick

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:12.970
in a little village.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.620
This is one of those stories
about war where no one dies,

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:16.570
no one even gets hurt,

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.360
and, for a little while,
there's this bit of light

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.100
in the middle of something terrible.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.930
So, World War II, 1941.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.840
Britain is being bombed
every night by the Luftwaffe.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.040
Over in Germany, William Joyce
broadcasts fascist propaganda

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.680
over the radio to the UK,
trying to demoralise the British public.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.930
The press nickname him Lord Haw-Haw

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.290
because of his affected
upper-class accent.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.700
Sometimes, Lord Haw-Haw would forecast

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.040
which towns and places
would be bombed next,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.450
and he was eerily accurate.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.730
And on one of his broadcasts,
he threatened Linby Docks.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.273
Dockyards were strategic bombing targets;
of course they were.

00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:54.400
But Linby Docks aren't
dockyards at all.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.823
They're these little streams
that run through the village of Linby.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.500
The docks are just a local name.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.070
But Luftwaffe commanders
had looked at the map,

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:09.260
seen Linby Docks, and decided to waste
some of their limited war-fighting resources

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.020
on bombing something that,
while quite pretty,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.160
was strategically useless.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.870
Oh, and they missed.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.500
The bombs landed a couple of miles away.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.050
No one was even slightly hurt,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.890
although presumably one farmer's field

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.540
had some unexpected and
rather violent ploughing done to it.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.270
It's a lovely story.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.200
It's part of the local
mythos here in Linby.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.790
You'll find articles about it online

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.350
and it's referenced on the
lovely little parish map

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.890
that is next to the bus shelter.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.590
And I'm really, really sorry to Linby,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.350
but I'm not sure it's true.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.890
Because if you look at
the maps from back then,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.020
they didn't say 'Linby Docks' on them.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:44.853
Of course they didn't.

00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:47.900
These streams are so small that
they are barely even marked

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.600
on the largest-scale maps.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.170
If Germany was using maps
that were that detailed

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.060
and that precise, well,
then they could tell

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.510
that the docks weren't big
enough to have any ships in them.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.020
And if they weren't
using maps that detailed,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.780
well then the docks wouldn't
be marked on them at all.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.000
Lord Haw-Haw, that propaganda broadcaster,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.080
he didn't actually give away secret plans

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.853
or announce bombing raids in advance.
Of course he didn't!

00:02:09.853 --> 00:02:11.910
The Luftwaffe didn't want
to give advance warning

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.600
so they could be shot down.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.050
So, he just lied or relied on reports

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.120
that came back in the news

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.980
in the hope that he would be right enough

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.460
to convince the British public,
who weren't fools.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.870
A lot of folks tuned in to him,
a few million,

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.600
but it was mostly for entertainment.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.020
There's an argument that
rather than hurt morale,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.480
he actually helped British solidarity.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.090
And there aren't many recordings of him;

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.000
there's certainly no audio of him
saying 'Linby Docks' that I could find.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.980
The Blitz was logged in detail
by all the local authorities

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.510
and all the records were compiled.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.330
Hucknall, the nearby town,
is mentioned twice,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.720
but Linby isn't mentioned at all.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.450
I searched through all the
relevant local newspapers,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.900
they've been digitised,
and they have no articles

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.410
about any bombings near here.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.963
I even asked the local historical society,
and they were wonderful,

00:02:57.963 --> 00:03:00.610
they went through a lot
of documents for me.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.780
But there's nothing from
the time that backs it up,

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.520
just folklore and stories
told from parent to child,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.750
from elder to youth, for
maybe the last 80 years,

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.100
which does hurt a little, because
it's such a wonderful story.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.050
Now, if I present something
like this to the world

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:24.530
and say, "I don't know," there's a tendency
for certain overenthusiastic people

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.150
to see it as a mystery
and try to solve it.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.930
The only people who know for sure
are over 90 years old

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.700
and, look, no one should bother them

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.950
trying to disprove or prove
one bit of local folklore.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.420
Besides, I'm only in my thirties and
I've occasionally had moments

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:40.120
where I've recalled
clear memories of things

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.193
that evidence later showed me
I have had completely wrong.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.120
But the bomb records are not perfect.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.550
Newspapers back then might
not have mentioned it

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:54.340
because "Bomb Lands in Field,
No One Hurt" is not a story,

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.770
at least not in 1941; it would be today.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.000
Maybe it did happen.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.030
Or maybe someone came
up with a story once,

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.997
told it as truth, and everyone thought,

00:04:01.997 --> 00:04:04.920
"Sure, that sounds fun, let's go with it."

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.110
I am expected to provide
evidence for claims, rightly so.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:11.000
But there are countless
tales that never happened.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.160
We call them urban legends
or eventually just legends.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.860
Did King Alfred really burn the cakes?

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.110
Probably not. Does it matter?

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.520
Over a long enough period
of time, no, not really.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:23.820
I don't think something like this

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.193
needs to be proven or debunked,
even if it somehow could be.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.833
It's just a good story.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.430
Thanks to the historians and archives
who helped me with my research.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.230
I have put links in the description.

